HolRefute by example
====================

These examples showcase typical HolRefute usage: what a user actually types
while developing a proof.  They are documentation that happens to be
executable, not a regression suite; the selftest in `..` is what pins
behavior.

The main HolRefute interface is its proof tactics.  All files except example
11 are modern HOL4 theory scripts built around:

    REFUTE_TAC
    QUICKCHECK_TAC
    NARROWING_TAC
    MODEL_REFUTE_TAC

They state conjectures as theorems and invoke a diagnostic tactic in the
proof.  A tactic prints its result and returns the original goal unchanged.
In an interactive proof, inspect the report, repair the statement, and try
again.  Deliberately false theorems in this executable corpus use:

    REFUTE_TAC >> cheat

The `cheat` only admits the goal after the diagnostic has printed; it is not
part of normal use.  When a conjecture is true, the diagnostic tactic is
followed by an ordinary proof of the unchanged goal.

`REFUTE_TAC` is the normal first choice.  The other three tactics select a
particular search family or backend and are useful when that choice matters.
These introductory tactic examples intentionally use the adaptive defaults:
size and carrier-cardinality search starts with window 10 and deepens while
the shared 10-second call budget remains.  Tools requiring an exact frontier
should use the fixed `upd_size` or `upd_card` updaters, as example 11 does for
its bounded automation profile.  Because the defaults adapt, the scopes
reached and the counterexamples printed depend on the machine; read the
reports, do not expect a fixed transcript.

Example 11 is deliberately the only exception.  It explains the SML-level
automation API for tools that need per-call configurations and structured
outcomes.  The other examples contain no direct calls to `refute`,
`quickcheck`, or `model_refute`, and no SML `val` declarations.


Running the examples
--------------------

From `src/HolRefute`, build every example with:

    ../../bin/Holmake examples

To build one example, run Holmake in this directory, for example:

    ../../../bin/Holmake -C examples refuteExample01Theory.uo

Examples 01, 02, 04, 05, 09, 10, 12, 13, and refuteGrammarScript.sml
contain MODEL_REFUTE_TAC calls.  They need Kodkodi 1.5.7 and a Java runtime:

    export HOL4_KODKODI=/path/to/kodkodi-1.5.7

Without a configured model finder, MODEL_REFUTE_TAC reports an inconclusive
search and still leaves the goal unchanged.  The deliberately false example
theorems remain buildable because their following `cheat` admits that goal.
`NoCounterexample` itself does not imply that Kodkodi ran: exhaustive QC and
narrowing can also prove that a finite search space is completely covered.


The files
---------

refuteExample01Script.sml
    The normal proof-development workflow, starting with REFUTE_TAC.

refuteExample02Script.sml
    Choosing the general, executable-only, narrowing-only, or model-finder
    tactic.

refuteExample03Script.sml
    User-defined datatypes, recursive definitions, records, options, and
    pairs.

refuteExample04Script.sml
    Assumptions, implicit and explicit quantifiers, and predicates.

refuteExample05Script.sml
    Inductive and executable predicates used as assumptions.

refuteExample06Script.sml
    Narrowing over functions and partially demanded values.

refuteExample07Script.sml
    Datatype invariants expressed as assumptions.

refuteExample08Script.sml
    Using counterexamples to add missing hypotheses and spot unnecessary
    ones.

refuteExample09Script.sml
    The finite model finder on functions, sets, and relations.

refuteExample10Script.sml
    Structured finite models for datatypes, inductive relations, and nested
    quantifiers.

refuteExample11Script.sml
    The sole SML-level automation example: configurations, outcomes,
    certificates, goals, formatted results, and unused-assumption checks.

refuteExample12Script.sml
    Executable and relational support for standard-library types.

refuteExample13Script.sml
    Machine words: the finite carrier at a concrete width, widths left as
    type variables, and the operations that are not encoded.


Case studies
------------

These examples adapt case studies from Isabelle's Nitpick manual and its
Nitpick_Examples and Quickcheck_Examples suites.  They use deliberately
small carriers or executable fragments so that the counterexample search
stays readable while exposing a realistic modeling or implementation error.

refuteQueueScript.sml
    A two-list functional queue models its representation invariant,
    abstraction, and enqueue/dequeue operations.  The draft enqueue omits
    re-normalization, and QUICKCHECK_TAC exposes the resulting empty-queue
    and observer failures; this is in the style of Isabelle's
    Quickcheck_Examples.thy.

refuteAATreeScript.sml
    An AA tree models levels, well-formedness, rotations, and insertion.
    The draft insertion omits skew and split, and two plausible claims about
    the corrected insertion -- one on levels, one on well-formedness -- are
    false as well; QUICKCHECK_TAC and REFUTE_TAC carry the three
    refutations.  The case study adapts section 3.2 of Isabelle's
    Nitpick manual, Manual_Nits.thy.

refuteAVLScript.sml
    An AVL rotation model tracks ordering and cached heights.  Its draft
    double rotation swaps the grandchild subtrees, breaking order;
    NARROWING_TAC builds the offending subtree, and the fixed rotation is
    proved by case analysis.  This adapts Isabelle's
    Quickcheck_Narrowing_Examples.thy.

refuteHotelScript.sml
    A finite trace model covers hotel guests, key cards, room locks, and
    occupancy.  A stale-key behavior lets a guest enter without being the
    owner, and QUICKCHECK_TAC builds the trace that shows it.  NARROWING_TAC
    then refutes the stronger claim that a room its owner may consider safe
    is occupied only by that owner, and is the diagnostic on the payoff
    lemmas, which are proved by induction.  It adapts Isabelle's
    Quickcheck_Examples/Hotel_Example.thy.

refuteSubstScript.sml
    A de Bruijn term model defines lifting and two versions of substitution.
    The draft uses the wrong cutoff under a binder, and NARROWING_TAC finds
    a closed term it changes; REFUTE_TAC checks the corrected substitution
    before its general proof.  The example adapts section 2.10 of Isabelle's
    Nitpick manual, Manual_Nits.thy.

refuteGrammarScript.sml
    Four mutually inductive grammars model successive repairs to a grammar
    for balanced words.  MODEL_REFUTE_TAC exposes the three production bugs,
    while a mutual-induction proof establishes soundness for the corrected
    grammar.  This adapts section 3.1 of Isabelle's Nitpick manual,
    Manual_Nits.thy.

refuteSecTypeScript.sml
    A loop-free IMP fragment models expressions, commands, evaluation, and
    low-observable states in the Volpano-Smith setting.  The draft checker
    forgets the guard's security context, so REFUTE_TAC exposes an implicit
    flow; QUICKCHECK_TAC then searches the corrected statement, which
    ordinary lemmas prove.  It adapts the security-type-system case study
    from the Nitpick ITP-2010 and LPAR-2010 papers.

Read the numbered tutorial files first, then these case studies in the
order listed above.  The tutorial files introduce the tactic families and
the case studies show how the same diagnostics scale to data structures,
programming-language definitions, and inductive relations.


Further reading
---------------

`../README` describes the backends, trust model, configuration, and known
limitations.  The public SML interface is in `../Refute.sig`; tactic users
normally need only `REFUTE_TAC` and occasionally one of its three
specialized variants.
